00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:52.280
I just logged in
about 20 minutes ago.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:54.158
So with the Cloud
Access Manager,

00:27:54.158 --> 00:27:55.450
there's three kind of concepts.

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:59.440
There's deployments, connectors,
and remote workstations.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:01.280
Remote workstations
I'll get to in a second.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:05.560
But with deployments, say
you're a managed service

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:08.380
provider where you're
managing different customer

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:10.325
accounts on their behalf.

00:28:10.325 --> 00:28:12.700
In that case, you would want
to have multiple deployments

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:16.510
per customer where you'd
have a different AD

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:20.360
and different cloud credentials
to provision those machines.

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:23.800
Another example of where you'd
want multiple deployments is

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.650
say you want an area for
your sandbox environment

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:30.100
so you can do all your sandbox
testing before modifying

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:32.030
your production environment.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.150
Another example is
disaster recovery.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:37.090
You might want to set up an
entirely different deployment

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:40.200
for your DR site.

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:42.450
And then, connectors, these
are the three connectors

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:45.250
that I was showing in the
architecture diagram earlier.

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:50.220
We've got the Vancouver
on-premises one, the GCP US

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:54.890
East, and the GCP US West.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:57.140
And you can monitor the
health of the connector,

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:59.210
especially if the
connector goes down.

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:01.028
And then, from the
connector, there's

00:29:01.028 --> 00:29:02.570
a couple different
things you can do.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:05.450
You can create a new
remote workstation.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:07.070
Creating a remote
workstation is where

00:29:07.070 --> 00:29:11.840
you would provision a
remote workstation where we

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.243
would do that on your behalf.

00:29:13.243 --> 00:29:14.660
And we would install
the software,

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:16.490
install the NVIDIA
drivers if you

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:19.070
decide to use a
graphics agent machine,

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:24.540
as well as actually
add it to the domain

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:27.060
such that you can
have your users log in

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.910
with a single sign-on
user experience.

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:31.410
So I'm just going
to kind of quickly

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:34.300
show what it looks like for
the simple user interface.

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:38.710
So here, I can select a
Windows or a graphics machine.

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:40.710
I'll just enter my name.

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:44.660
And then, since I've already
set this up with GCP,

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.770
here are all the different
regions and zones

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.480
that you can select from.

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:50.250
I'm just going to
